package org.mattshoe.mtg.core

internal val setLorwynEclipsed = StorySection(
    "ecl", "Lorwyn Eclipsed",
    "Forty years after the faerie queen fell, day and night no longer take turns on Lorwyn-Shadowmoor, they share the land, and four students from Strixhaven tumble through an Omenpath into the middle of it.",
    listOf(
        StoryArticle(
            "ecl-the-plane", "The Plane",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-a-return", "A Return",
                    listOf(
                        "Lorwyn Eclipsed came out on January 23, 2026, a week after its prerelease weekend opened on January 16 and three days after it reached Magic Arena. Its set code is ECL, and the two Commander decks that came out beside it carry the code ECC. It was the 109th Magic expansion, and it took the game back to the plane of Lorwyn-Shadowmoor for the first time since 2008, when Shadowmoor and Eventide closed out the old block. The original Lorwyn of 2007 is where the planeswalker card type began, with Ajani Goldmane and [Liliana Vess](who/liliana) among the first five, and both of them turn up again here.",
                        "The return was first teased at Gen Con in 2023 for late 2025, then pushed into early 2026 once Universes Beyond sets began landing in Standard. Mark Gottlieb led the vision design and Michael Majors led the set design, with Deborah Garcia as art director, and many of the artists who painted the first visit were brought back to keep the look true. The idea the whole design began from was simple. Lorwyn and Shadowmoor, which used to take turns over centuries, now exist at the same time, side by side. So about half the set leans on Lorwyn's creature-type families and the other half on Shadowmoor's colours, hybrid mana and -1/-1 counters.",
                        "In the story it sits in the last arc of the tale that followed the Phyrexian invasion. The dragonstorms of [Tarkir](tarkir-dragonstorm) have only just been stopped, the [Omenpaths](the-omenpaths) are still opening wherever they please, and one of them drops four first-year students from Strixhaven into a meadow on Lorwyn. What they find there leads straight into the next set, Secrets of Strixhaven. To mark the release, Wizards worked with the Jim Henson Company on a short film set on the plane and played entirely by handmade puppets.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-day-and-night", "Day and Night",
                    listOf(
                        "Lorwyn is the plane's daytime face. It is a land of idyllic meadows and green forests that have never known night. The sun sits high for most of the day and, though it sinks low, never goes out. Humble granges dot the countryside and small hamlets mix their peoples together. The tamed lands were long called the Blessed Nation, after the supposedly blessed caste of elves who claimed them, though the name fell out of favour after the Pendulum Year. Everything wilder and further off is the Primal Beyond.",
                        "Shadowmoor is the night. Its forests are dark and its brambles thick, and the moon circles the sky through an endless imitation of a full night, waxing and waning but never setting. Great stones lie under moss, carved with the symbols of peoples long since unmade, and bogs that never stop burning swallow careless travellers. It is dangerous, and it is also full of life that thrives in the harshness. Every dark pass hums with wild magic, a power that anyone can draw on and that is chaotic enough to destroy both the caster and everything around them when it goes wrong.",
                        "What makes the plane unlike any other is that it holds two planar aspects at once. Once the Great Aurora kept them apart, shifting the whole world from one to the other about every three hundred years. Now they lie against each other like the swirls of a marbled cake. A sunlit path in Lorwyn can end at a wall of gnarled darkness, and in Shadowmoor a hedge of thorny moonlit brambles can turn, across one line, into a flowering green bush. The borders creep like tides, and sometimes a whole stretch of land flips in a moment. Deaths and births can push them, the footsteps of Eirdu and Isilu can push them, and often they move for no reason anyone can see, as the plane corrects its own balance.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-eventide", "Eventide, Morningtide",
                    listOf(
                        "Everyone born on the plane has two selves, a Lorwyn self and a Shadowmoor self. Crossing from Lorwyn into Shadowmoor is called Eventide, and crossing back is Morningtide. Nobody changes species in the crossing, but body and mind both change. Lorwyn folk tend to be communal and orderly, Shadowmoor folk individual and wild, and neither side owns good or evil. Anyone can be a hero or a villain depending on where they stand.",
                        "Memory is the cruel part. Step over the line and your life in the other aspect blurs into something like an itch you cannot reach, while the mind quietly rewrites your past to fit your new self. A kithkin who had spent ten years in Shadowmoor and stepped into the light would suddenly believe her whole childhood had been spent in Lorwyn. Step back and the true memories return. People know this well, and plenty who are curious about their other self never go to meet it, because meeting it would cost them the life they know.",
                        "The story shows it happening to a hero. Brigid Baeli of Kinsbaile, fighting from a strip of sunlight in Goldmeadow, is dragged into the dark by kithkin who already have yellow moon-bright eyes. She struggles, goes still, and opens her eyes full of that same light, then turns her bow on the people she was defending a moment before. The card {Darkness Descends} carries the scene in its flavour text, Isilu's presence turning day to night, fellowship to suspicion and neighbours against one another.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Who you are at night and who you are in the day are two different people.",
                        "Brigid Baeli, Lorwyn Eclipsed Episode 3",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-eclipsed-realms", "The Eclipsed Realms",
                    listOf(
                        "Where the two aspects grind against each other, a third thing can form. These pockets are the eclipsed realms, a state that is neither light nor shadow, where the plane tries to settle the mirror quarrel between Lorwyn and Shadowmoor along some other axis entirely. Some are thin slivers along a border and some cover great swathes of land. In them the sun and the moon hang locked in place in the same sky, and every shadow holds a beacon of light and every sunbeam a well of darkness.",
                        "An eclipsed realm does not flip a person the way a border does. A traveller who enters one keeps their memories, and slowly starts to recover the memories of their other self too. That sounds like a gift and is the opposite. The two sets of memories try to merge and cannot, and as the contradictions grind on, the traveller loses desire, feeling and any tie to the world outside, until they stop moving entirely. Such a creature is called a calciform. The card {Nameless Inversion} describes these places as raw, undefined boundaries where the plane naturally corrects imbalances of power and influence.",
                        "For forty years after the Great Aurora ended, the eclipsed realms were mostly rumours from distant villages, places where neighbours and homes had been caught between light and dark and had become something that was both and neither. Now they are a fact of life. In the story Star-Crossed, an elf named Rhain is wounded in one and spends years slowly turning to stone from three claw marks, and a whole meadow of snaplilies near his village has been lost to another.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "A reliquary is needed to pass through the eclipse safely. Without it, two warring selves will calcify into one.",
                        "Liminal Hold",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-reliquaries", "Reliquaries",
                    listOf(
                        "There have always been a few ways around the change. Long ago there was the Crescent of Morningtide, a relic empowered by a great elemental that could carry its bearer across the aspects unchanged. Today the people of the plane make reliquaries out of pieces of Eirdu and Isilu themselves. A Reliquary of Dawn, made from Eirdu, lets its bearer resist Eventide. A Reliquary of Twilight, made from Isilu, resists Morningtide. Carrying both is said to let a person walk through an eclipsed realm untouched. They are rare and often sacred, shaped as lanterns or pendants according to the piece they were made from, a feather becoming a brooch or a glowing scale set behind glass.",
                        "The story finds other ways. Ashling the flamekin was touched by an elemental, and though her body still flickers between flame and rime, her heart and memory no longer change, so anyone who walks in her light keeps something of their daylight self. The merrow who guard the sleeping Eirdu stay the same however hard night falls, so long as they stay near it. The elves of Lys Alana march into the dark carrying reliquary torches that hold captive sunlight elementals, little figures pressing their hands against the flames to get out.",
                        "Outsiders are the strangest exception of all. People from other worlds do not change between night and day. Kirol, a vampire from Strixhaven, walks into a Shadowmoor grove and out again with their memory whole, which is exactly why High Perfect Morcant wants them. In the side story Star-Crossed, an elf from Shadowmoor named Cerys crosses into Lorwyn with her uncle's failing Reliquary of Twilight, and when its light gives out the change takes her almost at once.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Lorwyn Eclipsed"),
                StorySource("Story", "Planeswalker's Guide to Lorwyn Eclipsed, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Lorwyn Eclipsed Episodes 1 to 7, by Seanan McGuire, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Lorwyn Eclipsed"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "ecl-the-carriers", "Eirdu and Isilu",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-eirdu", "Eirdu",
                    listOf(
                        "When Oona fell and her Great Aurora ended, two vast beings woke in the sky above the plane. Eirdu is the living embodiment of Lorwyn and the sign of the sun. It walks on six legs, its fur red and white, its folded wings at its back and a long tail of thorns behind it, and a gleaming sun hovers above its head. To stand near it feels like a soft breeze and the gentle warmth of spring. It is restful for body and spirit, and leaves a person content simply to be alive.",
                        "Some suspect the pair were never new at all, that they always lived on the plane and had only returned from the Primal Beyond. Either way they are stewards, not rulers. While one is awake the other is meant to sleep, and the waking one wanders and spreads its domain. Eirdu has never needed to defend itself, but if threatened it could bring down the ruinous heat of the sun in pillars of endless light. In the story it lies curled in the courtyard of Glen Elendra, small enough to fit, and when Maralen lays her palm against its muzzle it treats her as an old friend.",
                        "Eirdu has worshippers who would turn the whole plane into Lorwyn. They wear armour marked with sunbursts and wings, and on cold days, when the sun shines less warmly, they hold the Festival of Idyllglare. A pyre is built inside an old dolmen or at the heart of a village, a costume shaped like Eirdu is set on an elemental and paraded round it, and the faithful feed the flames with crops and heirlooms until the elemental collapses or nothing is left to give. The card {Eirdu, Carrier of Dawn} gives every creature spell convoke, and {Sun-Dappled Celebrant} shows the dancing.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Come into the light, strange one. All are welcome to bask in Eirdu's glow.",
                        "Kithkin rite-leader, Springleaf Parade",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-isilu", "Isilu",
                    listOf(
                        "Isilu is Shadowmoor and the moon. Its hide is a deep midnight blue fading to silver-gold near the head, its neck impossibly long, and its wings are fused behind it so they drag like a vast tail. Where the head should be there is a cluster of drifting cloud around a softly glowing moon, and its eyes are two smaller moons, cratered and without pupils. Being near it feels like calm silence and the chill of autumn, bonfires far off, crisp leaves, sweet cider, and it fills people with understanding, so they find themselves seeing answers to questions they never knew they had.",
                        "If threatened, Isilu could pour out a night no light could pierce, choking the will to live from everyone inside it. Darkness is not its wake but its herald. It runs ahead of the beast and trails behind it, and wherever it falls Lorwyn becomes Shadowmoor. Its tail leaves furrows that fill at once with glowing moonflowers and starlight buttons. The card {Eirdu, Carrier of Dawn} turns into Isilu, Carrier of Twilight for a single black mana, and on that face every other nontoken creature you control has persist.",
                        "Isilu's adherents would make the whole plane Shadowmoor. They wear moon motifs and clouded headwear and keep vigil over their dead in the burial places under the great stones. Every species, whatever its creed, holds those grounds as places where no violence is done, and an elemental draped in a costume of Isilu stands at the entrance to show a rite is under way. {Moonlit Lamenter} shows them keening out their grief, and {Moon-Vigil Adherents} names their claim on the whole world.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Isilu's presence turned day to night, fellowship to suspicion, and neighbors against one another.",
                        "Darkness Descends",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-firdoch", "Firdoch and Mysteries",
                    listOf(
                        "The two carriers have servants of a kind. Since they emerged, stone creatures called firdoch have begun wandering the land. They serve the elemental guardians in ways nobody understands, carving paths and managing the borders between Lorwyn and Shadowmoor through elaborate wrestling. The flavour text of {Firdoch Core} catches one waking, half-buried stones stirring as Isilu drifts past in a swirl of shadow and moonlight.",
                        "The pair are greater elementals, the plane's giant embodiments of ideas, and they are not the only new one. When Oona was struck down, a curious entity was born alongside the new age, the {Mass of Mysteries}. It is the potential of the unknown, and it walks the plane turning the certain into the uncertain. Where it steps, fallow fields sprout flowers never seen before, rivers flood with fish and then run dry, and people who were happy in their lives leave home to find something more.",
                        "Lorwyn's greater elementals look like animals stuck together without rhyme or reason. Shadowmoor's look like nightmares made from the fears of a harsh land, and they are unfeeling and indifferent to everyone around them. None of them are good or evil. The flamekin can talk with them after a fashion, though the talk is mostly instinct and guesswork. As for Eirdu and Isilu, they seem to care for balance and nothing else. The story's climax turns on that, when the two of them meet in the middle of a battlefield and do not fight at all.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Lorwyn Eclipsed"),
                StorySource("Story", "Planeswalker's Guide to Lorwyn Eclipsed, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "The Legends of Lorwyn Eclipsed, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Lorwyn Eclipsed Episodes 1, 2 and 6, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Lorwyn Eclipsed"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "ecl-history", "A History",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-age-of-oona", "The Age of Oona",
                    listOf(
                        "For millennia the plane belonged, in secret, to Oona, queen and mother of all faeries. Her Great Aurora was a magical convergence of her own making that preserved her body and memory across the changes and gave her control of the world. Every three hundred years the plane flipped to its other aspect and everyone in it forgot. Ashling describes it plainly in the story. Oona caught the small auroras that naturally flare between night and day and braided them into one, so night never fell and day never rose, and a whole people meant to live in balance was stopped where it stood and left to stagnate.",
                        "Under her the elves of Lorwyn were the power in the land. They ruled in clans, each with its own brand of cruelty, and boggarts were their favourite prey. Oona played the peoples against each other. Her first attempt at an heir, a shapeshifting son made to rule Shadowmoor, rebelled against her. Her second she slipped into the shed skin of a Mornsong elf named Maralen, meaning to pour herself into the girl when the world turned. That plan failed too.",
                        "Some forty years ago the cycle was disrupted by the Great Mending, the healing of the time rifts across the Multiverse that is told in [The Mending](the-mending). For the first time in ages Oona feared the Aurora might catch her too. When it came and turned the plane to Shadowmoor, the girl she had made had become someone of her own, and she joined a band of unlikely allies to bring her maker down.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-pendulum-year", "The Pendulum Year",
                    listOf(
                        "The band that ended Oona was Maralen, the elf hunter Rhys, the kithkin Brigid, the merrow Sygg, the flamekin Ashling and the treefolk sapling Colfenor. Oona's body was destroyed. Her consciousness, the Planeswalker's Guide says, was not, and it remains hidden, slowly regaining strength while she looks for a way back to her throne. Maralen took up the crown of the fae and bound Rhys to herself with a death pact. If any sign ever showed that Oona was returning through her, he was to kill her, and until that day he could not die.",
                        "Then the two halves of the world began to fuse, and the time that followed is called the Pendulum Year. Pockets of Lorwyn sprouted in the dark and pockets of dark in the light. Villages split down the middle, and anyone crossing a new border changed on the spot. Farmers were cut off from their fields or forgot their trade, and neighbours could not reach each other. For a time there was even something like days and nights, as the land swung back and forth.",
                        "Slowly the swinging settled. Borders inched like gentle tides instead of crashing waves, and people began to see two great shapes walking far off along the horizon, Eirdu and Isilu, the twin worldsoul of the plane come back to govern its auroras. For the next forty years there was a fragile peace. The only troubling tales came from distant villages that had been caught between light and dark and become something else, the first word of the eclipsed realms.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-the-invasion", "The Invasion",
                    listOf(
                        "The [Phyrexian invasion](invasion-of-the-multiverse) broke that peace. The elves of Lorwyn were nearly wiped out, and their old dominion was turned into a network of power for the invaders. The compleated elves kept their cruelty and their prejudice, and the oil hardly changed how they treated other peoples. The elves who stayed clean were driven back until, in a desperate gamble, they allied with the other peoples of Lorwyn and finally made a stand at Lys Alana. The Phyrexians answered by infecting kithkin and using the thoughtweft to poison their comrades' minds, and by sending corrupted faeries to taint the water.",
                        "In Shadowmoor every people fought in its own way. That was less effective than Lorwyn's united front, but cost far fewer lives. The boggarts rode their cavalry into the enemy lines as the vanguard and died in the numbers they expected. When Halo poured across the Multiverse at the end, it was the isolationist kithkin of Shadowmoor who led the last charge, and a young kithkin known as the Hound of Bristlebane, riding a springjack, took the belief and fear of her whole people and drove a spear through a Dominus from neck to knee as she leapt along a branch of the Invasion Tree.",
                        "The scars are everywhere in the cards. Lorwyn kithkin who cut themselves off from the thoughtweft to escape the oil now wander as nomads. Flamekin who burned out in the war slept underground, and many woke as rimekin. The elves of the Gilt-Leaf mark the scarred parts of their forest as too ugly to travel, according to the decree on {Scarblade Scout}. {Bristlebane Outrider} patrols kithkin lands still haunted by memories of oil and blood.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "I don't care what the others say. Luck left us during the Invasion. The only thing we can trust now is our own grit.",
                        "Surly Farrier",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-tenuous-peace", "A Tenuous Peace",
                    listOf(
                        "The present is a peace held together with string. Villages can still flip without warning, and their people may never know they once lived a different life. The eclipsed realms are spreading, and nobody understands them. Yet much is better. The elves' empire has shrunk in land and influence, and without Oona setting the peoples against each other, trade flourishes, with each people selling what it does best.",
                        "The plane has no native humans. It belongs to kithkin, boggarts, elves, merrow and the humanoid elementals called flamekin, rimekin and cinders, with giants, treefolk, changelings and noggles seen more rarely, and bogles, ouphes, duergars, hags, imps and trolls in the far reaches of Shadowmoor. Since the Omenpaths opened, it has outsiders too. Brigid tells the students that strangers have been tumbling into Lorwyn ever since the metal invasion cracked the shell of the world, and that river crews and elf hunters keep turning them up.",
                        "Under the calm, old powers stir. High Perfect Morcant of Lys Alana wants to end Shadowmoor and bring back the empire. Maralen's skin is slowly hardening into a faerie's carapace, and she fears what that means. Some faeries still worship Oona and work at bringing her back, and many Lorwyn faeries follow a self-proclaimed queen, Oura, whom Maralen will not destroy for fear of becoming like her maker. That is the world the Strixhaven students fall into.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Lorwyn Eclipsed"),
                StorySource("Story", "Planeswalker's Guide to Lorwyn Eclipsed, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Lorwyn Eclipsed Episode 5, If We Shadows, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Oona"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Lorwyn Eclipsed"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "ecl-kithkin-boggarts", "Kithkin and Boggarts",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-thoughtweft", "The Thoughtweft",
                    listOf(
                        "Kithkin are small folk who value community and live simple lives in villages called clachans in Lorwyn and douns in Shadowmoor. They are farmers at heart, but can do heroic things when danger comes. What binds them is the thoughtweft, an empathic web joining the minds of every kithkin. Through it, kithkin near each other share feelings and thoughts. It is not a hive mind. Each kithkin keeps their own thoughts, but each is tuned to everyone else's emotions.",
                        "In Lorwyn the web feeds friendship and openness, and individual wants give way to the good of the whole clachan and even its allies. In Shadowmoor the same web stokes fears of betrayal and demands sameness. No secret can be kept in it, and anyone who tries to hide their thoughts is exiled or worse. Shadowmoor kithkin once called it the mindweft, though the word is falling out of use as they seek sameness even across the border. {Kinbinding} says it simply, mind to mind and heart to heart. {Timid Shieldbearer} says the other half, that one kithkin's panic can move a whole doun.",
                        "The invasion turned the web into a wound. At first the kithkin used it as a weapon, a lone village calling for help across the plane so that Phyrexians found themselves flanked before they knew it. Then the oil got into one kithkin and every other one heard the voice of the Mother of Machines and their own compleated friends promising them perfection. Many cut themselves off entirely. Brigid's own link is mangled and half severed, she tells the students, and it makes the people of Goldmeadow nervous to have her stay long.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "During my time on the road, the emptiness I felt after severing myself from the thoughtweft has faded with every story heard and every tale spread.",
                        "Goldmeadow Nomad",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-clachan-and-doun", "Clachan and Doun",
                    listOf(
                        "Lorwyn kithkin trade grain and produce for iron tools, bottled spells and art for their homes. Under the elves they were not the main target of cruelty, but they still longed to live without its shadow. Since the war, integration is the new way of the clachans, and the Book of Kith and Kin, which holds the gathered heritage of all kithkin, keeps gaining new passages as other peoples move in. They are deeply superstitious, reading omens in a rake turned the wrong way, and most of all in the comings and goings of the greater elementals, which is why their augurs matter. The ones who cut themselves from the thoughtweft could not settle back in, because the cries of the compleated still echoed through it, so they formed nomad bands that wander looking for work and meaning.",
                        "Shadowmoor kithkin wall themselves in behind iron gates and stone ramparts. Inside, oddly, they are equals. Each month a tribunal lays bare a candidate's every deed and secret, and the one chosen becomes the Sul, the eyes of the people, locked in a tower called an Inslintur that looks out over the whole doun. Nobody ever knows whether the Sul is watching them. When trouble comes they will send one of their own out rather than open the gates, a hero filled with the whole community's belief, and such a hero is said to be able to face fifty boggarts and live.",
                        "They also build scarecrows, usually from the wood of dead treefolk. The scarecrows have no real mind and keep slaughtering the people who made them, yet the douns keep building them, because fear of outsiders forbids the safer course of making friends. {Reluctant Dounguard} is the sort of kithkin who only leaves the walls to keep others out, and {Foraging Wickermaw} is one of the scarecrows. In the Commander decks, {Dread Tiller} comes with a kithkin wickersmith's warning not to build it from blighted wood.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-boggart-magic", "Boggart Magic",
                    listOf(
                        "Boggarts are the plane's goblins, and they live for new sensations. Magic builds up in a boggart's body as it ages, so the eldest are the most powerful. When a boggart dies the body goes into a swamp, where it becomes part of the place, and those swamps grow rich in magic, and whatever is gathered there becomes a focus for spells. A focus can be a relative's bones, the shell of a dead pet turtle, or a particularly good stick. {Unbury} puts it more bluntly, that a well-aged boggart body is steeped in magic richer than any faerie glamer.",
                        "In Lorwyn their magic is ritual, full of parables, limericks and convoluted, arbitrary steps. The Boon of the Spider is the famous example. Catch a spider, whisper it a story under the new moon, pull off its legs, seal the body in a hollow acorn with a drop of your blood, and wear the acorn for protection against hexes. In Shadowmoor the magic in their bodies grows volatile the more they have, the dead are eaten whole for their power, and they practise cursecasting with gruesome ingredients, a goat's heart stuck with porcupine quills or the cooked guts of a toad that swallowed its own young.",
                        "After the elves culled them in the years before the invasion, Lorwyn boggarts lost their feasts and most of their old stories. The war, oddly, saved them. The elves fought in the open and died of their arrogance, while the boggarts held their swamps with magic and ambush. Afterward they began feasting again, found that the surviving parables had drifted and changed, and started writing new magic down in a simple picture-script scratched on bark and stone, signs for danger, safety, food and, most often, adventure awaits.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "This bone is a good bone. Very angry. I can make lots of pain with this one.",
                        "Boggart Cursecrafter",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-the-aunties", "Warrens and Aunties",
                    listOf(
                        "Lorwyn boggarts live in small warrens led by an auntie, one of the oldest and most magical boggarts, whose word can break up any fight, though friendly biting is allowed. They roam the plane hunting new experiences and come home to share them, and they lose limbs and lives doing it. Death is not feared or mourned. A death, even a foolish one, is celebrated if it happened in pursuit of something new. In the story the students shelter with the Stinkdrinker Warren in a mangrove swamp on the Wanderwine, where the boggarts haul Sanar off to learn eel fishing and come to help ferry the travellers downriver.",
                        "Shadowmoor boggarts were once driven by hunger and violence alone, but the fall of Oona, the elves and then the invasion made that life impossible, and they have learned caution, if not restraint. Their warrens are small, practical and well hidden, and warriors who do well hammer scavenged iron plates straight into their bones. When boggarts fight each other, the winner strips the plates off the loser. No Shadowmoor warren has ever lasted more than a generation, and no other people has bothered to write their history down.",
                        "The greatest Shadowmoor aunties do not stay with any warren. They wander alone, fickle and cruel, and spring strange tests on anyone they meet, tests that often have no right answer. Fail, and you get an ironic and disturbing curse. Please them, and once in a while you get a great gift. The set is full of aunties, {Heirloom Auntie}, {Elder Auntie} and {Sourbread Auntie} among them, while {Warren Torchmaster} names Auntie Soot, whose raids last exactly as long as the oil burning on top of her head.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "C'mere you little urchins. Auntie's trying a new recipe, and I need some tasters.",
                        "Sourbread Auntie",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Lorwyn Eclipsed"),
                StorySource("Set", "Lorwyn Eclipsed Commander"),
                StorySource("Story", "Planeswalker's Guide to Lorwyn Eclipsed, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Lorwyn Eclipsed Episodes 2, 3 and 5, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Lorwyn Eclipsed"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "ecl-merrow-elves", "Merrow and Elves",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-merrow-lanes", "The Merrow Lanes",
                    listOf(
                        "Merrow are the plane's merfolk, its merchants, couriers and go-betweens. They hold the rivers and underground channels known as the Merrow Lanes, and though they move poorly on land, everyone depends on them to carry goods and news. Their magic is the glamer, which changes how they look and sound. A colour glamer can hide them or draw the eye, and a voice glamer can quietly nudge a land-dweller's feelings during a bargain. Other merrow hear right through it, so among themselves glamers are a second layer of speech that only merrow perceive.",
                        "Lorwyn merrow are an aristocracy built on fair trade, polite and haughty at once, sticklers for rules they often invented for themselves. They live in schools, self-contained cliques each with its own look, customs and glamers, and each school has its elite, chosen by virtue and the ability to lead rather than by birth. Rival elites can hold conversations that sound like small talk and are in fact vicious personal insults. They keep decorated storefronts along the riverbanks, and they despise anyone who gets rich by fraud. The invasion found them believing water would keep them safe, and many had to flee into the deepest stretches of the Wanderwine.",
                        "In the story the merrow are kind. When Abigale is swept down a tributary, the fish-finned people who meet her sign to her slowly, and her Silverquill hearing aid turns their signs into words in her mind. They ask whether she is an elemental beast, warn her the waters are not safe now that so many of their kind have changed over to the night, and take her to see what they have been guarding, the sleeping Eirdu.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Other species may spread across the land all they like, but the riverways and the skies above them will always be ours.",
                        "Tributary Vaulter",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-dark-meanders", "The Dark Meanders",
                    listOf(
                        "Shadowmoor merrow are con artists and proud of it. They are not the strongest people on the plane, but they think they are the cleverest. Their voice glamers lure and command the weak-minded, and their illusions, made from water magic, look like coloured mist on close inspection. In the Age of Oona they drowned land-dwellers and even dragged themselves ashore to steal. When the Phyrexians came none of their tricks worked, and they fled into the Dark Meanders, tunnels far beneath the plane, to wait the war out. Living down there changed them, and many came back glowing in the dark or carrying poison in their bodies from what they had eaten.",
                        "Their favourite scams are worth knowing. Pay me now and I will pay you back double, then run. Buy this potently magical charm, which vanishes the moment they are out of sight. Best of all, a fake boggart trinket with all the magic and none of the boggart nastiness. When they find a truly weak will, they charm the victim into helping, and when the con is done they tell the victim to walk into the water and drown. The loners, roguish or obsessive, can leap rivers, run the Meanders blind and pop out of wells.",
                        "The cards show both halves. {Encumbered Reejerey} is a Lorwyn merrow whose worth to her school is measured by the wealth she brings home, and {Silvergill Peddler} is a merchant mentioning that another clachan was very interested in this item. On the dark side, {Spell Snare} says Shadowmoor merrow swindle not only for profit but for spite, and {Gravelgill Scoundrel} sings a little rhyme about cutting the line, taking the catch and snatching a kithkin.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-gilt-leaf", "The Gilt-Leaf",
                    listOf(
                        "The elves of the plane love beauty, their own and the world's, and they live short lives with great intent. In Lorwyn that love has always curdled into contempt. Most Lorwyn elves are cruel believers in breeding out the lesser, and anyone they think ugly is an eyeblight. Their old caste system put only the most flawless at the top. After the invasion, castes collapsed in all but name and became simply hereditary, and the elves now live in fiefdoms, each paying tribute to High Perfect Morcant at Lys Alana, each with its own customs and sometimes its own secret claim to the throne.",
                        "Lys Alana is the heart of the Gilt-Leaf Wood, a city of boardwalks, towers and tree-trunk doors, high walkways of wood and vine, all made from trees whose scaled bark is sealed with a sap that blazes gold in the sun. To an elf it is the most beautiful city in the world, and Kirol, marched there at spearpoint, can see why. The elves who live there wear the city's colours so well they vanish when they stand still.",
                        "Lorwyn elves grow moonglove and distil it into moonglow, a poison strong enough to drop a giant, and the perfects of the Dawn's Light Palace have made the flower holy. It appears in the royal regalia as a reminder that the empire has shrunk but its blades are still deadly. {Moonglove Extractor} calls distilling it a revered but fleeting position. Today the elves must cooperate with their neighbours to keep their comfortable lives, so most hide their hatred and point it at Shadowmoor instead, hoping that if the night is ended under their rule, their empire will come back.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Despite recent alliances, we must never forget the ideals of perfection which define us.",
                        "High Perfect's decree, Morcant's Loyalist",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-safewrights", "Safewrights",
                    listOf(
                        "Shadowmoor's elves are the same people turned inside out. They are protectors of beauty in a world of indifference and malice, keepers of a few well-stocked safeholds, the best-preserved cities in Shadowmoor. Their worth is measured by effort and devotion to beauty, not rank, and they count heroism itself as beautiful. During the invasion they fought a ruthless guerrilla war until their safeholds fell, and survived on great caches of beautiful and powerful objects they had gathered from across the plane.",
                        "Safewrights roam Shadowmoor seeking out beautiful things to protect, and whenever land turns from Lorwyn to Shadowmoor they go looking for whatever beauty it holds. Bloomseekers study Lorwyn itself, and they alone may carry Reliquaries of Twilight across the aurora. Their tales hold whole firesides spellbound. Shadowmoor elves do not scorn other peoples, but they do believe themselves morally above them, and they pity their Lorwyn counterparts as sad and disappointing, held back by their prejudices.",
                        "Their great treasure is dawnglove, the mirror of moonglove, which once grew only in the darkest depths of Shadowmoor. Distilled, it becomes dawnglow, which can cure almost any ailment, even death, yet it is so beautiful that the elves almost never spend a petal. Picked, it keeps its form across the aurora, which makes it priceless in Lorwyn. That is the flower the whole main story turns on. Rhys's dagger carries moonglow, Morcant wants dawnglow turned into a poison for Isilu, and Kirol picks the dawnglove that saves a queen.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "I will endure grasping vine and prickling bramble to preserve even one flower.",
                        "Creakwood Safewright",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Lorwyn Eclipsed"),
                StorySource("Story", "Planeswalker's Guide to Lorwyn Eclipsed, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Lorwyn Eclipsed Episodes 3, 4 and 6, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Lorwyn Eclipsed"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "ecl-flame-and-fae", "Flame, Fae and Giants",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-path-of-flame", "The Path of Flame",
                    listOf(
                        "Flamekin are elementals of flame and living stone with a deep bond to the plane and the gift of speaking with the greater elementals. They prize becoming who they truly are. They gather in small towns called flickers that never grow big because their people keep wandering off, and they meet up at festivals in calderas and burnt-out pits, usually at the sites of elemental shifts. They are smiths, messengers and investigators, and their forge-homes make iron for the rest of Lorwyn, though the elves always hated them for fear they would burn the forests.",
                        "The great quest of a flamekin's life is the Path of Flame, the physical and spiritual work of self-discovery. It starts with soul-searching, moves through travel and learning, and finally shows itself in red, yellow and white flame, through three states called incindiator, blazeform and soulstoke. Each step needs wisdom, self-knowledge and time spent in meditation with the flame inside. Old rumours spoke of a fourth path, and many flamekin now assume the rimekin are it. Ashling left the Blessed Nation at the start of the Pendulum Year to look for that fourth path.",
                        "In the war many flamekin chose to burn out gloriously rather than flee, and many more went underground to sleep. Some have not woken yet, and some flamekin spend their lives searching for them. The card {Enraged Flamecaster} shows one who recognised the weapons of a brother lost in elven lands, and {Kulrath Zealot} one whom the Path gave power at the cost of who he was.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "You seek guidance on your Path. Then let me provide it: What greater flame is there than the sun?",
                        "Flamebraider",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-rimekin-cinders", "Rimekin and Cinders",
                    listOf(
                        "Rimekin are new. They were born from flamekin whose feelings burned too hot during the horrors of the invasion. Unable to hold their fire, they turned it inward and put it out, collapsing like stars into cold logic, and became heatless reflections of themselves. They think about the state of the world and how to change it, and the weapons they forge freeze even flamekin iron. About half the flamekin who slept through the war woke as rimekin. When Ashling steps into Shadowmoor in the story, blue light races over her skin and her fire turns to something frozen and shimmering like an aurora, and she tells the students that by night she is what they call rimekin.",
                        "Shadowmoor's original flamekin were cinders, skeletal smoky husks who could barely hold a flame. They say their fire was stolen long ago in the Extinguishing, which set them on the Path of Sorrow, a spiral toward self-destruction in the hope of rekindling. The invasion forced them to face themselves. They found a vicious joy in fighting the Phyrexians, and many realised their power could shape a world they wanted to live in. Some became rimekin, others went back to Lorwyn to win back their warmth, and the rest build larger towns than Lorwyn's and forge weapons for every corner of Shadowmoor so that Shadowmoor will need them.",
                        "In the story, the cinders of a Shadowmoor grove are banked shapes with ember eyes that chase Kirol out of the dark. {Rimekin Recluse} explains the rimekin, and {Summit Sentinel} shows their guards on the slopes of Mount Tanufel, the flamekin mountain where the Wanderwine is born.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Rimekin were once flamekin who turned their heat inward following the horrors of the Invasion. They burn cold, choosing isolation as the path to preserve their flames.",
                        "Rimekin Recluse",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-the-fae", "The Fae",
                    listOf(
                        "Faeries are fickle pranksters who live for petty intrigue, love dreams and work in illusion. All of them came from Oona. Lesser fae are born from flowers and live about three years, they feed on dreamstuff pulled from sleeping minds and trade in secrets, and they have no use for anything as silly as a job. Even the most carefree turn irritable if parted from their clique and desperate if lost from it. They change little between the aspects, a little crueller in their pranks by moonlight, and they live in Glen Elendra, a valley hidden by the queen's glamers.",
                        "Glen Elendra in the story is a wonder. Inside a briar hedge whose thorns slither like eels, a single huge flower rises on a braid of stems as thick as a kithkin's thigh, and inside its silver, gold and ivory petals stands a palace of moulded bramble, sized for elves and run by faeries no taller than a hand. Patches of day and night dapple it. The faeries fly to keep to one or the other, and there is a third, faerie light from flowers that glow in the dark patches.",
                        "Maralen is now the great faerie who rules there. Her personal flock numbers thirty-five, one for each day of proper swarming season, and when the leaf-brown faerie Camey reports that Aherin is missing and somebody else has been sleeping in his place, it is the first sign of the trouble. Not every faerie loves the new queen. Some worship a self-crowned upstart named Oura, and some still work to bring Oona back. {Glen Elendra's Answer} carries Maralen's own claim on her home, and {Bitterbloom Bearer} is the set's headliner, a faerie that breeds more faeries every turn.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "This is my domain, and I am its Queen.",
                        "Maralen, fae ascendant, Glen Elendra's Answer",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-giants-treefolk", "Giants and Treefolk",
                    listOf(
                        "Giants spend most of their lives asleep and rarely deal with smaller peoples. Lorwyn giants are aloof wanderers, thought wise for their travels and sought out for their knowledge and their hoards of baubles, and territorial wherever they settle. Any shock, of grief or of plenty, can send a Lorwyn giant into name sleep, and young giants take their surnames from those sleeps. A giant marks its resting place with a dolmen, a great stone gateway visible from far away, and dolmens stay up after the giant leaves, so an old one can mean a living giant or an empty lair full of treasure. Shadowmoor giants hibernate for weeks, grow moss, and stride right over villages without noticing.",
                        "Treefolk are the oldest people on the plane. They begin as ordinary trees until an event called the Rising wakes them. Young Lorwyn treefolk stay in their groves learning from elders, then go out to guide those who need them, though some, in older times, ruled as tyrants. Shadowmoor treefolk have mostly lost their faces and become lonely, vengeful guardians of nature. {Doran, Besieged by Time} is the old wise treefolk of the first visit, now trapped in Shadowmoor, voiceless and afraid to step back into the light in case his memories do not survive it.",
                        "The giants' cards are full of jokes at small folk's expense. {Hovel Hurler} says giants love two things, sleeping and getting rid of whatever wakes them. {Boldwyr Aggressor} remembers Clan Boldwyr's legendary defence of their dolmens in the war. {Giantfall} sums up the danger in two sentences, the giant bumped and the hamlet was gone.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-changelings", "Changelings and Kin",
                    listOf(
                        "Changelings are shapeshifters born in the crystal cavern of Velis Vel. They are terrible at disguise, because their skin is opalescent and see-through, but their shifting lets them bond with every other people. Apart from the elves, nobody minds them, and since they proved loyal allies against the Phyrexians they are counted lucky. If one is driven into Shadowmoor it becomes a mimic, a cruel thing that wears a victim's loved ones' faces before killing them, so since Oona's fall they keep to Lorwyn. A kithkin saying on {Graveshifter} uses them to mean somebody will be remembered.",
                        "The set's commons are full of little changelings caught mid-transformation, each named in the same pattern of adjective and creature. {Chitinous Graspling} watched a shiny object from afar and found a form to reach it, {Prideful Feastling} is a friendly changeling whose shape remembers carnage, and {Mischievous Sneakling} believes surprise is the most important part of making friends.",
                        "The plane has smaller oddities too. Noggles have no idea of money but a strong taste for anything shiny, as {Noggle Robber} admits, and ouphes like {Glister Bairn} leave things lying about to see who comes to take them. Bogles, duergars, hags, imps and trolls live mostly in Shadowmoor's far reaches, but as the tides shift, people have begun reporting them in Lorwyn too.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "We'll see a changeling at their funeral",
                        "Kithkin saying, Graveshifter",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Lorwyn Eclipsed"),
                StorySource("Story", "Planeswalker's Guide to Lorwyn Eclipsed, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "The Legends of Lorwyn Eclipsed, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Lorwyn Eclipsed Episodes 2, 5 and 6, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Lorwyn Eclipsed"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "ecl-a-field-trip", "A Field Trip",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-harriers-wood", "The Harrier's Wood",
                    listOf(
                        "The story, written by Seanan McGuire in seven episodes from December 8 to 16, 2025, begins on Arcavios. A teaching assistant named Dina leads a first-year class in Introduction to Magibotanical Environments an hour's walk from Strixhaven to the Harrier's Wood, far enough from campus magic that the snarlflowers there grow clean. Underclassmen have only been allowed there for two years, since the Oriq and their mage hunters were driven off. Each student must gather six to ten unblemished blossoms to feed a new litter of Witherbloom pests. Dina, bored, leans against a tree and thinks that babysitting is at least better than the years when friends died or turned into Phyrexian horrors.",
                        "Four students drift together. Abigale is a brown-feathered owlin, deaf, who signs and whose Silverquill hearing aid echoes her words into others' minds. Kirol is a vampire who climbs through the branches to sneak up on her, which annoys her a great deal. Sanar is a small blue goblin on the Prismari track who somersaults out of the bushes with an empty basket. Tamira, Tam, is a green-striped gorgon whose basket is nearly full and who hands Sanar some of her flowers. Then Sanar sees a little blue creature in the trees, insect-like with a strangely human face and wings like flakes of mica, and it laughs and flies off.",
                        "Sanar runs after it carrying Tam's basket. Abigale and Kirol chase Sanar, and Tam chases her grade. None of them look down. A root unwinds across the path, they tumble over a rise into a hole ringed with perfect snarlflowers, and fall into prismatic light. The little creature giggles and dives in after them.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "I don't think we're going to make it back in time for Introduction to Prophecy.",
                        "Tam, Strixhaven first-year, Evolving Wilds",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-isilu-wakes", "Isilu Wakes",
                    listOf(
                        "They land in a meadow of spiralled wildflowers and floating stones under a triangular gap in the sky. Sanar notices that one of the suns is missing, and Tam works out that the other one is back on Arcavios where it belongs. The Omenpath is fifteen feet up and only Abigale could reach it, and she refuses to leave them. Behind them stands a dolmen, two great spiralled stones with a third across the top, and through it lies total night. Tam reasons that whoever built a gate that tall could boost them to the portal, and they go through.",
                        "Beyond the gate a tunnel runs down past cave paintings of two six-limbed winged beasts, one with a sun for a head and one with a moon, taking turns at sleeping and watching. Kirol is still admiring them when the others stop dead. In a vast round chamber lies the moon-headed one itself, asleep, smelling of autumn and bonfires. Sanar cannot help himself. He walks into the circle, lays his palm on its mossy neck, and feels the greatest peace of his life. Then Isilu opens its eyes and roars.",
                        "The roar is every terror of the night. Isilu stamps, and a wave of near-solid darkness rolls out of the tunnel and across the meadow, and wherever it passes the sun becomes a thin ring of fire, the moon swells full, grass dies and other flowers spring up glowing. On a standing stone the little blue faerie throws its arms wide as the dark washes over it, and it changes. Its face hardens, its eyes go large and gold, its wings fray like autumn leaves, a crown-like crest rises on its head, and its blue turns to gold-flecked green. It giggles, delighted, and flies off after the night.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-glen-elendra", "Glen Elendra",
                    listOf(
                        "The students flee the dark from one patch of daylight to the next until a stout archer waves them over. She is Brigid Baeli, hero of Kinsbaile. She tells them that strangers have been tumbling into Lorwyn ever since the metal invasion, and that Isilu is supposed to be asleep, not stomping through Lorwyn seeding Shadowmoor in their territories. When Abigale speaks into her head, Brigid winces and asks her to stay out, since she is not part of the thoughtweft. As they watch, the spreading night sweeps over the Omenpath and wipes it from the sky.",
                        "Far off in Glen Elendra, the green faerie squeezes through the briar hedge, steps into sunlight and turns blue again, plainly disappointed. It flies to the queen's chamber and drops a sprig of foxglove on her pillow. Maralen wakes from a terrible dream. Her flock reports that one of them, Aherin, is missing and that something has been sleeping in his place. Rhys comes in, a glamer hiding his broken horns, and when Maralen glances at her mirror she sees a small figure made of flower petals glaring back at her with endless menace, and hears laughter nobody else can hear.",
                        "She flees to the courtyard, where Eirdu lies curled in pure daylight, and rests her hand on its muzzle. Then the day elemental rises and walks away, and patches of night begin to open in the courtyard behind it. The little blue faerie dives into one of them, turns green and giggling again, and follows the queen. Its business in Glen Elendra is not done.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-news-for-liliana", "News for Liliana",
                    listOf(
                        "Back on Arcavios, Professor [Liliana Vess](who/liliana) is enjoying her free afternoon when someone knocks outside office hours. It is Ajani Goldmane. She knows at once that someone has died. It is [Jace](who/jace). Ajani explains that after he helped Elspeth and Narset stop the [dragonstorms](tarkir-dragonstorm), they followed the signs to the Meditation Realm and found Jace there, no longer Phyrexian but wrong somehow, casting something nobody could name. He lost control of it, and it took him apart into light and energy. Liliana refuses to believe he is dead without a body.",
                        "Then Ajani tells her the rest. When Jace let Liliana go on Ravnica, he had also let [Ugin](who/ugin) capture [Nicol Bolas](who/nicol-bolas), and let everyone believe the dragon was dead. The storms tore through the Meditation Realm, and Bolas escaped through an Omenpath. Nobody knows whether his spark survived, and he does not know where Liliana is, but she needed to know. Her teacup shatters on the floor.",
                        "At that moment Dina bursts in. Four students are missing, they chased a strange flying bug-like creature and fell into an Omenpath that closed behind them. Liliana says there is nothing like that around Strixhaven. Ajani asks Dina to show them where it happened. In the wood, Liliana's necromancy finds no dead students, and Ajani finds a stitched blue petal and knows its scent from a plane where flowers are worn as clothing. There is a stable Omenpath from Lorwyn to Shandalar, he says, and from Shandalar he can bring them home. He takes two steps, glows, and is gone.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "In one breath, Abigale's mind turned from panic to poetry.",
                        "Unexpected Assistance",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Story", "Lorwyn Eclipsed Episode 1, Out of This Wood, by Seanan McGuire, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Lorwyn Eclipsed Episode 2, Shake Off Slumber, by Seanan McGuire, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Lorwyn Eclipsed Episode 3, Aweary of This Moon, by Seanan McGuire, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Set", "Lorwyn Eclipsed"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Lorwyn Eclipsed"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "ecl-day-into-night", "Day Into Night",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-goldmeadow", "Goldmeadow Falls",
                    listOf(
                        "Brigid takes the students to Goldmeadow, a walled kithkin town in a meadow of standing stones, and seats them in the meeting hall. She does not live there, she explains, because her broken link to the thoughtweft makes the locals nervous. When they describe the creature they followed, she knows it for a faerie, and she happens to be a close friend of the faerie queen. Then Sanar asks why they do not just ask the faerie, because it is hovering right in front of him. It laughs, louder and louder, until alarm bells drown it out, and it is gone.",
                        "Isilu is walking through the fields outside, and the night is pouring over the town. Where it touches, the walls grow taller and sprout spikes, ladders vanish, windows grow bars and doors narrow. Brigid is stunned. Goldmeadow has been on the Lorwyn side for years, and kithkin nomads are inside the walls, welcome by day but not by the kithkin of the night. She has promised to stay with the students and cannot. She gives them directions, east to the Wanderwine, across it to the Gilt-Leaf Wood, along a tributary to a waterfall and beyond and behind it, then up a stream to Glen Elendra. Then she warns them that whoever begs them to turn around will not be her.",
                        "They run. Abigale carries Sanar into the air while Kirol and Tam race down a street half in sun and half in shadow. Kithkin with yellow cat's eyes throw stones and loose arrows from the dark, and the daylight kithkin, open and trusting, are taken from behind one by one. From above, Abigale sees Brigid shooting to wound, not kill, until the dark slithers along the wall behind her and pulls her in. Afterward, Abigale signs that the kithkin of the night did not seem evil, only frightened, like cornered pests. The night is not bad, she thinks, only different.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-lys-alana", "Lys Alana",
                    listOf(
                        "Abigale flies the others over the Wanderwine, and on the far side Kirol, who reads landscapes for fun, notices the flowers glow faintly even in the sun and guesses that is how they live through Shadowmoor. In the Gilt-Leaf Wood Kirol lags behind, and a silent hunter pulls them into the bushes with a knife at their throat. He is Lluwen, an elf with dainty hooves and curling horns, of the Nightshade pack. He saw them flee Goldmeadow, he believes they know why Isilu walks, and he is taking his prisoner to High Perfect Morcant. Kirol warns him that they are not a good prisoner, and Lluwen admits that some people say he is not a very good hunter.",
                        "On the way they argue about hiking shoes, and whether hooves count as feet. Lys Alana stuns Kirol. High Perfect Morcant is so flawless that Kirol nearly bows under her gaze. She tells them that Isilu should not wake for six months, and that waking it by touch alone is impossible, then explains the world in her own words. Day is perfect, beautiful and faultless, night is wicked, twisted and bitter, and the two beasts walk where they please, ignoring treaties and unmaking oaths.",
                        "Kirol notices this is not quite how Brigid told it. Morcant waves the kithkin away as simple folk who only see what is in front of them. Then she drifts off into her own thoughts. Isilu is out of cycle and perhaps vulnerable. Kill the night and the elves need never again undo by moonlight everything they achieve by day, and the Gilt-Leaf Empire could rise again. The weapon will be dawnglow, she decides, healing so potent it would be death to the night, mixed with summer dreams and bound in a tincture. In the doorway Lluwen goes pale. Kirol asks to leave. Morcant smiles a terrible, perfect smile and says they are not going anywhere.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Morcant's voice was as sweet as honey and as dangerous as moonglove.",
                        "Perfect Intimidation",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-the-river", "The River",
                    listOf(
                        "The others only notice Kirol is gone once they are deep in the wood. Tam wants to press on for the sake of the project and getting home. Abigale, feathers puffed, signs that Kirol is their friend and storms back, and Sanar follows her. A massive beast with antlers of tangled branches rises out of the brush. Abigale cannot hear it. Sanar charges it with a stick and is flung into the trees, and its bellow sets off the alarm setting of Abigale's hearing aid so loudly she reels dizzy into the very tributary Brigid told them to find.",
                        "Her soaked feathers drag her under. Abigale forces herself to be calm, owlin have air sacs that buy a little time, and since all her magic needs is her hands, she gathers the winds into a bubble and holds it to her beak as a mask. She lets the current take her toward the Wanderwine and its shallows. Then shapes with spears and tridents swim out to meet her. She babbles an apology with her hands, and one of them signals her to slow down, and signs back.",
                        "Meanwhile Sanar runs back to Tam scratched all over. Tam has to choose. Chase Abigale, or reach the faerie queen and warn her about the beast spreading night. She thinks of her teacher, whom she never talks about, who would want her to protect herself and save the most people. So they find the tributary and follow it up to a waterfall, behind and beyond as Brigid said. Sanar plunges straight through and does not come back out. Tam touches the cool, sweet water, thinks that her classmates need her, and steps through.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-rhys-promise", "Rhys's Promise",
                    listOf(
                        "In her chamber Maralen is on her knees among drifts of small fragrant petals that come from no flower in Lorwyn or Shadowmoor. She knows them. They are the petals of Oona's flesh, the stuff Maralen herself was born from before she wore the shape and memory of Maralen of the Mornsong. If they are here, she fears, the twist of magic that let her become her own person is coming undone. Eirdu has left her, Isilu walks, day and night will go to war, and after war will come a new aurora, so reasonable and so rational, and it will make her Oona reborn.",
                        "Faeries bring in Tam and Sanar, found climbing the palace stairs. Maralen takes Tam for an elemental, and Tam explains she is a gorgon from Strixhaven who only wants to get back to school. Then Rhys slams the door open with a small blue faerie on his shoulder, the very faerie, Sanar shouts, that they followed through the portal. Rhys looks at Maralen with cold, terrible relief. She was keeping it secret, he says. He made her a promise long ago. He draws his dagger and lunges.",
                        "He slips on a petal. He catches himself with remarkable grace, slips on another, and stumbles straight out of a window, while Tam does something glowing and geometric with her hands. It was probability magic. Maralen tells the students that Rhys is her adviser and oldest friend and will kill her if he thinks Oona is coming back, because she made him swear he would. She is not ready to die, and if they can settle Isilu and restore the cycle she may not have to. Then she orders the giggling faerie onto her shoulder, and they run.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "What are the odds? And what would you like them to be?",
                        "Tam, Mindful First-Year",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Story", "Lorwyn Eclipsed Episode 3, Aweary of This Moon, by Seanan McGuire, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Lorwyn Eclipsed Episode 4, Fetch Me That Flower, by Seanan McGuire, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Set", "Lorwyn Eclipsed"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Lorwyn Eclipsed"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "ecl-the-long-night", "The Long Night",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-stinkdrinker", "The Stinkdrinker Warren",
                    listOf(
                        "Maralen cannot call her faeries without Rhys following, so Sanar packs the base of a giant flower bud with mud, crushed snarlflowers and a soggy flier for Mage Tower tryouts, and whistles. The bud explodes. The hollow stem drops them to the ground near the waterfall, and Maralen pulls a coracle out from among tree roots, where she has plainly kept it hidden for some time. Tam says what she sees. Maralen built a world she knew she could not live in. She explains that Rhys is an elf, and elves live only a blink. He has lived so long only because he is bound to her magic, and it seemed so unlikely he would ever have to keep his word that she let the promise stand to keep her friend.",
                        "The Wanderwine carries them for hours under a sun that never moves, past patches of shore where Isilu has stalked, until a vine draws the boat in among mangroves. This is the Stinkdrinker Warren, and here is Ashling, a lithe figure of flexible black glass with bonfire for hair and flame leaking from her joints. Sanar tells her she is beautiful, and she laughs. The boggarts take Sanar eel fishing while Maralen pours out her story.",
                        "Ashling already knows the elves plan to kill Isilu with poison. Tam asks whether that might not be good, since the world seems better in daylight. Maralen says no. Lorwyn is not meant to exist unchallenged, Isilu and Eirdu are equals, and eternal day is Oona's way, not hers. Ashling notes that Isilu would regenerate in time, as both elementals did after Oona destroyed them to make her aurora, but Maralen will not allow any attack on the natural order. So Ashling, sounding deeply put upon, says she will find them a better boat and come too. In the branches outside, the faerie flickers between blue and green, listening, perplexed.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Imagine the power of a flamekin and a rimekin united. Fervent passion confined by introspective insight. They would challenge every tale, rewrite every story.",
                        "Ashling, Twinflame Travelers",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-two-mistakes", "Oona's Two Mistakes",
                    listOf(
                        "On the boggarts' riverboat, Ashling tells Tam how Oona braided the auroras into one, and Maralen says flatly that Oona broke the world. Tam says that is no reason Maralen should die, just because Oona is her mother. Maralen corrects her. Oona was not her mother. She made her, pinching off a piece of herself like plucking a petal. Maralen began as a half-finished larva in Oona's bower, fed royal jelly, her wings plucked as they formed, kept as a weapon and not a child, meant to become Oona when the world rose up against her.",
                        "Oona made two mistakes, Maralen says. The second was her. Oona slipped her into the shed skin of Maralen of the Mornsong to rule Lorwyn as an elf, and never thought what an elf's heart might do to her plans. The first was her brother. Before Maralen, Oona made a son from the other half of the same petal, a prince in waiting for Shadowmoor. He fought her and demanded to rule by the natural cycle. He befriended a giant sage who told stories of Eirdu and Isilu, back when they were only legends, and called the giant father. Oona had the giant killed in front of them and told the boy that fathers always leave, fathers always fall. He swore never to forgive her or any part of her, and vanished into the green.",
                        "Tam insists none of it is Maralen's fault. The faerie on the table opens its mouth as if to speak, and then the boat runs aground. Ahead, a hard line of night slashes across the Wanderwine with auroras sparking along it. Shadowmoor has taken the river. The boggart at the helm says that all the new experiences in the world mean nothing if he cannot carry them home, and the boat stops there. Ashling says they will walk, and that anyone who stays in her light will keep something of their daylight self.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "She made only two mistakes, my maker-mother, and I was one of them.",
                        "Maralen, Lorwyn Eclipsed Episode 5",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-the-field", "The Battle in the Field",
                    listOf(
                        "While they travel, Ajani arrives at the dolmen gate into unbroken night, fights off a Shadowmoor serpent with millipede legs and then a Lorwyn boar with flowers on its back, both maddened by the sudden change, and finds Strixhaven shoeprints. Elsewhere Morcant marches Kirol, hands bound in elven silk, to a grove Isilu has already taken and sends them in for dawnglove, since they alone can cross unchanged. They find it glowing pink, purple and blue-white, pick a sprig, and outrun the ember-eyed cinders back into the light. Morcant orders them back in. Kirol refuses. Lluwen refuses too. Then a white lion lands between them, axe in hand, and asks whether Kirol is one of Professor Vess's students. Lluwen follows them into the forest.",
                        "In Shadowmoor, Ashling becomes rimekin, blue and frozen, and the moon-eyed kithkin of Goldmeadow, led by the night-turned Brigid, surround the travellers. Then Isilu comes walking toward them with a small green faerie at its head, and Maralen knows it at once. It is her brother. On the horizon a line of elven torches appears, and the kithkin close ranks against them. Isilu sniffs Ashling, then circles the little group protectively, snarling at the elves.",
                        "The elves charge, half of them carrying reliquary torches full of captive sunlight elementals. Kithkin arrows knock the torches loose, the freed elementals bow to Isilu and fly home to the day, and the elves who dropped them turn into their Shadowmoor selves and turn on their own side. Sanar fells an archer with a rock to the temple and knocks a reliquary from an elf's wrist. Tam stuns another with her gaze. Then Tam falls with an arrow in her belly, and Sanar tears off his sleeve and packs the wound around the shaft, remembering his first aid.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-the-prince", "The Prince Returns",
                    listOf(
                        "Rhys comes out from beneath Isilu with a dagger gleaming green with poison. He calls Maralen Oona. She does not run and does not raise her hands. She only whispers that it is not what he thinks. He cuts her arm, a shallow slice, and she bleeds petals and falls, and Rhys blinks as if a veil had lifted. She did not fight, she did not try to control him, she did not offer riches. She was not Oona.",
                        "The green faerie drops between them and grows as it falls, into a tall horned man with lake-blue forearms and the tatters of a prince's clothes. Oona is dead, he says, and Maralen is his sister. He had heard tales of Oona's fall from a traveller, come home, and taken the woman in Glen Elendra for Oona reborn in an elf's guise. So he tormented her, and when that did not work he brought outsiders to sow chaos. And she ran, for her world's sake, and proved she had never been their mother at all. When an elf's reliquary light falls on him he turns into his Lorwyn self, sharper and crueller, and turns the elf into an elk, and the rest of the detachment into rabbits.",
                        "Ajani vaults over the elven line carrying Kirol. The prince asks whether the Professor Vess the vampire mentioned is [Liliana](who/liliana), since they walked the planes at the same time. She knows him, he says, as Oko. The poison is moonglove and has no cure, Rhys says, but Kirol remembers from class that magical poisons have equal and opposite counters, and dawnglove is certainly magical. Oko becomes a faerie and flies off to find Morcant. Kirol and Sanar slip off after her too, and find her by Isilu's head with a vial of dawnglow. Sanar's rock spins into her shoulder, and Lluwen, horns grown thorny in the dark, catches her sword arm, headbutts her, and throws the vial to Sanar.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Kirol hated being a pawn in Morcant's game. If dawnglove was as potent as she suggested, it should be preserved, not used to tip the balance of power.",
                        "Kirol, Attentive First-Year",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Story", "Lorwyn Eclipsed Episode 5, If We Shadows, by Seanan McGuire, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Lorwyn Eclipsed Episode 6, Full of Hateful Fantasies, by Seanan McGuire, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Set", "Lorwyn Eclipsed"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Lorwyn Eclipsed"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "ecl-dawn-and-dusk", "Dawn and Dusk",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-eirdu-comes", "Eirdu Comes",
                    listOf(
                        "A belling cry rolls across the field, a whole dawn chorus pressed into one long note, and the fighting stops. A glow rises on the horizon like sunrise, and Eirdu lopes into view on its six legs, the sun above its head driving back the night wherever its light falls. Flying beside it is Abigale. Kirol waves at her like a madman, and Sanar reminds them she cannot hear.",
                        "Oko drops from the air and snatches the vial from Kirol, bows to the three of them almost mockingly, and flies off. Ajani has been trying to heal Tam and failing, the wound will not close however he works, and Oko thrusts the vial at him and orders him to save his sister. Ajani says he is busy with the girl. Oko explains why it is so hard. The magic of Lorwyn-Shadowmoor is fed by story, and the story they are in is one of death, damage and terrible sacrifice. If Ajani wants the girl to live, the queen must be saved first.",
                        "Rhys fears it is too late. Oko refuses. This is not how his little sister's story ends. He remembers, though creatures of Shadowmoor are not meant to remember their days, that his other self failed his son, failed every lover, and that they both failed their maker when they ran. Night or day, he will not fail his sister. He lifts her head and drips the dawnglow onto her tongue until she coughs, then swallows, then grabs for more with greedy gold eyes, and the wound on her arm stops bleeding petals and begins to bleed sap, and then plain red blood.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "What a cruel world, to force one such as I to play the hero.",
                        "Oko, Lorwyn Eclipsed Episode 7",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-the-dance", "The Dance",
                    listOf(
                        "Oko does not punish Rhys. He tells the elf he is already something he is not supposed to be, alive, and that a man who thought he was killing a tyrant has earned at least that much respect. When Lluwen stares and says he feels he should know Oko, Oko tells him everyone should, since he is the most important person the plane has ever produced. The last of the dawnglow he hands to Ajani, warning him not to mistake practicality for kindness, and Ajani pours it over Tam's wound.",
                        "Isilu rises, and Rhys fears the two elementals will crush them all if they fight. They do not fight. Eirdu bows, paws splayed and head to the ground, and Isilu bows back and pounces, and the two of them wrestle and circle like great cats at play, never touching the wounded or the fallen. Eirdu bends to Tam and breathes on her, a gust of summer afternoon, and the dawnglow in her wound flares purple and gold and she sits up coughing, and Sanar flings his arms around her neck. Abigale explains that the merfolk took her to the sleeping Eirdu, it smelled Isilu on her clothes and woke, and followed her all the way here.",
                        "Then Eirdu nuzzles Isilu's face. Isilu yawns enormously and turns back toward its cave, the spilled night unwinding behind it like ribbons, and wherever the night lifts, kithkin and elves turn back into their daylight selves, among them the fallen, gathered up by weeping kin. The elk stays an elk, and when an elf picks up a bow and aims at it, it bolts. Maralen, with twilight and dawn now in her veins, sees clearly. The night elemental woke because strange dreams hurt it, and it meant no harm. The elves fight the balance because it is their nature, and she will not punish anyone for following their nature.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "A lifetime of mischief gave way to a moment of tenderness.",
                        "Dose of Dawnglow",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-rhys-goes", "Petals on the Wind",
                    listOf(
                        "Rhys asks her to punish him and she will not. He kept his word, he says, and it killed him to strike her. Then Maralen realises with alarm that she can no longer feel the old promise. He is not bound to her magic any more. Ashling says it gently. Rhys is an elf of Lorwyn, and his season is a short one.",
                        "Rhys hugs her, which makes Oko flinch and threaten to give him four legs. He thanks her. He walks to Eirdu, who has sat down behind them, rises on the tips of his hooves and touches his forehead to the elemental's. He thanks Eirdu for its warmth and light, Maralen for her friendship and her hand, and all of them for saving her. Then he says he will be going now, and the wind takes him apart into petals, dawnglove and moonglove and apple blossom, each one leaving him paler, until Eirdu sits alone.",
                        "Maralen throws her arms around her brother. Rhys has gone because she did not need him any more, she says, and now she can rule as herself, unafraid. She asks Oko to stay and rule beside her. He says he loves his freedom too much, and that he gets to decide what he is meant for, not Oona. She tells him he will always have a home in Glen Elendra, because she does not like to be alone, and he takes her hand. Then, smiling and sorrowful like day and night together, she asks him what this is about a son.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-home-again", "Home Again",
                    listOf(
                        "Ajani leads the students to a stable Omenpath that clashes with the spirals of the land around it. Lluwen follows them out of the trees. He turned on his high perfect, and if he stays she will kill him. Tam suggests Strixhaven's cross-planar student programme, which is still taking admissions and would surely trade a scholarship for everything he knows about his plane. Ajani sighs that if Liliana kicks the elf straight back through the Omenpath, it is not his problem. They come out on a coastal road on Shandalar, and Tam gasps at being home. An hour's walk later they reach the path to Arcavios, and Abigale asks whether they are in trouble.",
                        "They are. [Liliana](who/liliana) tears into Tam behind her desk, warning her that next time she will not ask Goldmane for a favour, she will just leave Tam wherever she ends up, and that encounters with that bastard Oko rarely end well for anyone but him. Tam flees. Alone, Liliana rubs her temples and thinks that caring for others wears on you.",
                        "Then comes a knock unlike any she knows, firm and polite. At the door stands a tall woman in a tailored white gown and corset, black hair falling in waves too perfect not to be planned, smiling a warm, sisterly smile with Liliana's own lips. She steps past her into the office, touches her hand, and suggests she close the door, because they need to talk. The door shuts like a tomb, and the story ends there. In the next set, Secrets of Strixhaven, the students who came home from Lorwyn use the words Code Oona as their signal for help.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Story", "Lorwyn Eclipsed Episode 7, The Charm Dissolves Apace, by Seanan McGuire, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Set", "Lorwyn Eclipsed"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Oona"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Lorwyn Eclipsed"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "ecl-side-tales", "Side Tales",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-thieving", "Much Ado About Thieving",
                    listOf(
                        "Three side stories were published in January 2026. The first, by Tobi Ogundiran, is told by Auntie Grub herself to a hut full of young boggarts, chiefly a fidgety one named Nitwik. Four friends, Flib, Lugg, Ygril and Naance, set out to rob the lair of the mild giant Bre of Clan Stoutarm, only to find the border has moved and her lair is now in Shadowmoor, and Ygril will not cross without a Reliquary of Dawn. So she leads them instead to the dolmen of Boeb of Boldwyr, who is said to eat boggarts and make flutes of their bones. The promise of becoming more famous than Horp, the boggart who rides a giant hornet, wins the others over.",
                        "Past a tree hung with seven dead boggarts, they find Boeb deep in name sleep, ruddy and genial, smiling in his dreams. They strip the lair, ferrying loot away on their eagle Marrja, and Ygril pockets the stinkiest potions and eyes a phial labelled {Thousand-Year Elixir}. Flib climbs onto the giant's chest to hack loose an eternalisk scale hanging at his throat, an immortal creature's scale that could heal almost anything. Boeb's cloudgoat comes home bleating, its udders swollen after months without milking, Flib's axe drops into Boeb's chest, and the giant wakes and slaps Flib flat.",
                        "Ygril falls into Boeb's milk bucket, and when he sets it on the fire to boil she drinks a random potion and grows into a giant herself. The two giants brawl down the hill until her potion fades. Then Lugg and Naance come back on Marrja, and the eagle tears out Boeb's eyes. Grub reveals that Boeb fell into name sleep grieving his father, who had hunted the eternalisk scale for him as a boy, and that his smile was a dream of his father alive again. The friends share the loot with their swamp and celebrate Flib, who died foolishly but in service of something greater. Grub then shoos everyone out, worried about Nitwik.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-three-dreams", "Three Dreams",
                    listOf(
                        "Three Dreams, Three Truths, by K. Arsenault Rivera, opens on [Thunder Junction](thunder-junction), where Kellan, the son of Oko whose search for his father began in [Wilds of Eldraine](wilds-of-eldraine), gives his sweetheart Amalia a pot of meadowsweet from Lorwyn bought at a market. He has been dreaming of the flowers. She gives him a journal to write the dreams down. The dreams are a story happening on Lorwyn, three treefolk walking to plant a sapling, the scarred warrior ash Morecote, the earnest rowan Erewaker and the ancient elder Sigric, who carries the cutting grafted onto his own bark.",
                        "A faerie clique, Forget-Me-Not, Meadowsweet and Baby's Breath, goads Morecote into a duel. While he crushes Meadowsweet in his fist, the other two poison Sigric. Sigric fades into rambling memories of Colfenor, the old yew who favoured blackberries. Beside a sleeping giant the faeries strike again and wake her, and Morecote tears the sapling from Sigric's bark, shoves it at Erewaker and turns to face the giant alone. Sigric gives Erewaker a sheet of his own bark to plant beneath it, because yew grows best within a corpse, and calls up a wall of vines so she can run.",
                        "Alone, Erewaker crosses into Shadowmoor protected by a crescent-moon relic woven from Colfenor's branches, and reaches the grove where dawnglove grows by silver water. She performs the Rising, and nothing happens, and the faeries tell her it will only work if she casts aside her relic. She does. The sapling breathes, dawnglove swallows the grove and Erewaker with it, and the faeries die rejoicing, having fulfilled their purpose as the Deffroad Clique. Planes away, forget-me-nots bloom from Kellan's tongue, he coughs up a black flower, hears a woman's voice speak in riddles, and finally sees her face in his mirror ringed with black flowers. MTG Wiki identifies her as Oona, his grandmother, calling him home.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Home has its horrors. And its welcome. I await you with high hopes.",
                        "Lorwyn Eclipsed, Three Dreams, Three Truths",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-star-crossed", "Star-Crossed",
                    listOf(
                        "Star-Crossed, by Akemi Dawn Bowman, is a love story in letters. Cerys, a Shadowmoor elf, inherits the decaying house of her uncle Gethin, a bloomseeker who had sent her away as a child because she was unusually sensitive to the aurora. Among his things she finds an unsent parcel of balsam for a Mr. Rhain Breckon. Her letter, folded into paper wings, flies not toward the city of Caer Flur but toward the aurora. Rhain replies rudely, calling her uncle a coward. She replies sharply. Then she remembers how much appearance means to the elves of Lorwyn, and sends him nightphlox from outside her window, which grows only in Shadowmoor.",
                        "The letters soften. Rhain tells her he met Gethin the day the border shifted, when a boggart caught him and he stumbled deep into the eclipsed realm, and Gethin got him out and tended his wounds. Rhain does not tell her the rest. Three calcified claw marks run down his face and stony veins are creeping toward his heart, and only dawnglove can cure it. He begged Gethin for it and Gethin, unwilling to spoil a sacred flower, refused. Rhain would rather die than ask Cerys and lose another friend, so he writes that he wants nothing but her letters.",
                        "Then Cerys finds a stem of dawnglove under a floorboard with an unfinished letter. Gethin had picked it after all and never found the courage to send it. She writes asking whether Rhain is dying, and he answers that he is, and confesses he does not care what she looks like. She crosses with her uncle's broken reliquary and feeds him the dawnglow. He wakes. But her lantern fails, the Morningtide twists her into hatred, and calcification races over her. Rhain carries her back toward the border, and in the aurora she is stone to the waist. She tells him she finds his scars beautiful. He will not leave her, and they turn to stone in each other's arms, between worlds.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Story", "Lorwyn Eclipsed, Much Ado About Thieving, by Tobi Ogundiran, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Lorwyn Eclipsed, Three Dreams, Three Truths, by K. Arsenault Rivera, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Lorwyn Eclipsed, Star-Crossed, by Akemi Dawn Bowman, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, List of secondary characters, Lorwyn-Shadowmoor"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Oona"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "ecl-the-legends", "The Legends",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-old-heroes", "Old Heroes",
                    listOf(
                        "Maralen began as an avatar of Oona and is now the plane's steward alongside Eirdu and Isilu, speaking for its peoples and quietly guiding it from Glen Elendra. Her powers are weak but growing, and each day she comes closer to rivalling Oona. Decades of rule have slowly changed her body, her skin hardening into an opal-green faerie carapace, and that change is what frightens her into believing her maker is returning. Her card, {Maralen, Fae Ascendant}, is an Elf Faerie Noble that steals cards from an opponent's library whenever an elf or faerie arrives.",
                        "Rhys fought Oona nearly forty years ago and was kept alive by his pact with Maralen, carrying a moonglove-laced dagger and waiting for the day he would have to kill his friend. He is the only living elf who remembers the Age of Oona. His card is {Rhys, the Evermore}, and the story ends his evermore. Brigid trains the town watch at Goldmeadow and is gruff with anyone who comes for help, loath to remember her old traumas, though she is a true friend to those in need, and by night she becomes a closed, distrustful version of herself who is still not cruel. She remembers fondly her time as a pirate under Sygg. Sygg, another of Oona's foes, is now a schoolmaster with a new family, still a little sore that his old friends never gave back the Crescent of Morningtide, and by night he forgets his family and pines for piracy.",
                        "Ashling is a flamekin dancer and lifelong pilgrim. Her actions as the Extinguisher during the old story still haunt her. She went to the Primal Beyond seeking a way to reconcile her two selves and perhaps the fourth path, and came back understanding that the plane's duality is not something to fight but a sign that everyone contains multitudes. Three of them have transforming cards, {Brigid, Clachan's Heart}, {Sygg, Wanderwine Wisdom} and {Ashling, Rekindled}.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Maralen is more forgiving than Oona was, but her rule is no less absolute.",
                        "Mornsong Aria",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-oko", "Oko",
                    listOf(
                        "Oko was Oona's first avatar and, in her eyes, an abysmal failure. He was made to carry her will into Shadowmoor, but his Lorwyn side rebelled at once, and he turned out more of a trickster than even she imagined. He inherited her intellect and her love of deception, left the plane as soon as his spark ignited, and has been causing trouble across the Multiverse ever since. On Eldraine he was the father of Kellan, a son he never raised. In the story he tells Ajani that he and [Liliana](who/liliana) walked the planes at the same time.",
                        "The two halves of him differ sharply. His Lorwyn self is debauched and dangerous, the source of most of his mischief. His Shadowmoor self is calmer and more serene, and feels responsible for what has become of the plane in his absence. In the story his Shadowmoor shape is a tall man with a crown of twisted antlers and frozen-blue forearms, and when Lorwyn's light touches him his blue turns to summer sky and his voice turns crueller. As a faerie he is blue by day and gold-flecked green by night.",
                        "His card shows both sides. {Oko, Lorwyn Liege} is a blue planeswalker who gives creatures every creature type and shrinks attackers, and for one green mana at the start of your main phase it becomes Oko, Shadowmoor Scion, a green planeswalker who digs through your library, makes 3/3 Elk tokens and has an ultimate that pumps a whole creature type. Turning enemies into elk has been his habit ever since Throne of Eldraine, and he does it again to an elf in the story, remarking that there is not much difference between an elf of this land and an elk.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-morcants-court", "Morcant's Court",
                    listOf(
                        "High Perfect Morcant leads the elves at Lys Alana and carries her people's history on her shoulders. She is a tyrant who secretly longs to put the plane back under elven rule, and her methods are cruel and clever, setting peoples against each other with money, assassinations and misdirection. They bear little fruit, and suspicion closes in as her circle shrinks. When outsiders arrive she sees their worth at once and exploits them. Her card, {High Perfect Morcant}, makes every opponent blight whenever an elf of hers arrives.",
                        "Trystan is her poison master and horticulturist, raised on stories of the elves' greatness and their fall. He is frail, but his beauty is revered, and he rose quickly as her attendant and adviser. In truth he despises her and wishes he led the elves himself, and spends long stretches away from Lys Alana studying plants. By night he is weighed down by a guilt he cannot place and tends abandoned safeholds in solitude. His card is {Trystan, Callous Cultivator}.",
                        "Lluwen has always preferred animals to people. He can be reserved and rude but defends fiercely those he cares for. Born to elven supremacists and thought less beautiful than his peers, he had no interest in lost glory, but he feared the court too much to argue, so he played the obedient son and planned to leave. In the end he did, through an Omenpath to Strixhaven. His card is {Lluwen, Imperfect Naturalist}.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "I know I belong somewhere—just not here.",
                        "Lluwen, Imperfect Naturalist",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-elders-wanderers", "Elders and Wanderers",
                    listOf(
                        "Auntie Grub is a sage, matriarch and apothecary, the source of many of the parables boggarts hold dear. She and her frog familiar Smlurf have survived from the Age of Oona to now, and her survival lessons from the elves' culling and the Phyrexian war are used today in the casting of rituals. By night she is isolated and cruel, hoarding knowledge instead of sharing it, and her potions grow more potent and more deadly. Her card is {Grub, Storied Matriarch}. Doran, once the wisest of Lorwyn's treefolk, now wanders Shadowmoor voiceless as {Doran, Besieged by Time}. {Bre of Clan Stoutarm}, daughter of the giant Brion Stoutarm, is a herder who naps in her pastures and is at peace with every people except the elves she blames for her father's death, whom she hurls far, far away.",
                        "Ferrafor is the last yew treefolk. A cutting of Colfenor's sapling was saved in secret by Rhys, Colfenor's former apprentice, and nursed by an elder treefolk, and her waking was the bloody Rising told in Three Dreams, Three Truths. Raised in Shadowmoor without any treefolk to temper her, she is cruel and vindictive, sowing hexes and chaos, though in Lorwyn she would be cunning and kind, spreading life through the forest. She is {Ferrafor, Young Yew}.",
                        "Shadowmoor has its own terrors. Auntie Ool is spoken of only in whispers, a boggart who hands out potent, witty curses for the smallest slight, endless insomnia for the fool who woke her, a sentient and judgmental wart for the gossip who spoke ill of her. Her twig hut stands on giant frog legs and bounds silently across the wilds. The Reaper, if scarecrows can be said to have a king, is a shambling contraption whose origins nobody agrees on, and having lost much of its domain when the aspects merged, it now spreads blight hoping to rebuild its broken kingdom. They lead the Blight Curse deck as {Auntie Ool, Cursewretch} and {The Reaper, King No More}.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-first-years", "The First-Years",
                    listOf(
                        "Abigale is a deaf owlin with a gift for words that paint vivid pictures in other minds. She watches carefully, composes her thoughts with care, fights for others behind the scenes, and has a judgmental streak toward anyone who falls short of her ideas of justice. She has been bound for Silverquill since orientation, and is a poet who writes odes to the night sky over the Prismari campus. Her card is {Abigale, Eloquent First-Year}.",
                        "Sanar is a goblin who rarely finishes a creative project, flitting from one half-made design to the next, learning every new technique he can. People told him he would amount to nothing if he could not finish anything, but with a few friends' support he got into Strixhaven and flourished. He is on the Prismari track and blows things up when he needs to. Kirol is an athletic, inquisitive vampire who spent their childhood poking around ruins near home, the odd one out in a family of politicians, and after the invasion came to feel that preserving history mattered more than ever. They play Mage Tower and hope one day to fly. Their cards are {Sanar, Innovative First-Year} and {Kirol, Attentive First-Year}.",
                        "Tam, Tamira, is studious, unassuming and a little intense, treating Strixhaven as a grand thought experiment. She comes from Shandalar and wields paradox magic that can rewrite probability, though she has not mastered it yet. She has a teacher she keeps out of her thoughts so the mind-mages in her classes cannot see him, and even he sometimes flinches from her gaze. Her card is {Tam, Mindful First-Year}. Lluwen joins the four as their fifth, and the five rare hybrid legends together form a cycle in the set. Their professor is [Liliana](who/liliana), and their story continues in Secrets of Strixhaven.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "As Sanar watched the tangle of chirps and flames run by, his eyes lit up. \"I want one,\" he whispered.",
                        "Squawkroaster",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Story", "The Legends of Lorwyn Eclipsed, by Neale LaPlante Johnson, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Lorwyn Eclipsed Episodes 1 to 7, by Seanan McGuire, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Set", "Lorwyn Eclipsed"),
                StorySource("Set", "Lorwyn Eclipsed Commander"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, List of secondary characters, Lorwyn-Shadowmoor"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "ecl-mechanics", "Mechanics",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-blight", "Blight",
                    listOf(
                        "The new keyword action is blight. To blight a number, you put that many -1/-1 counters on a creature you control. It does not target, so you pick the creature as you do it, and you can put more counters on a creature than it can survive, so blighting three onto a 1/1 is a fine way to thank it for its service. Usually blight is a cost you pay, to cast a spell or activate an ability, though some cards make your opponents blight instead. {Soul Immolation} asks you to blight a big number and deals that much to each opponent and every creature they control.",
                        "Lorwyn Eclipsed is the first set since Hour of Devastation, almost ten years earlier, to make -1/-1 counters its main counter. There are no new cards in it that put +1/+1 counters on anything, but the rule still matters. When a permanent has both kinds, they cancel in pairs until only one kind is left. Plenty of cards remove -1/-1 counters, and those usually give a bonus on top. {Rhys, the Evermore} can strip every counter off a creature, and {Moonshadow} enters with six -1/-1 counters and sheds one each time permanent cards hit your graveyard.",
                        "The set also brings back persist, which returns a creature that dies with no -1/-1 counters on it, wearing one, and wither, which deals damage to creatures as -1/-1 counters. Proliferate turns up too, most notably on {High Perfect Morcant}, whose elves make each opponent blight as they arrive and can then proliferate those counters. {Spinerock Tyrant} is a dragon with wither that copies your single-target spells and gives them wither as well.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-vivid-hybrid", "Vivid and Hybrid",
                    listOf(
                        "The Lorwyn half of the plane is bright and colourful, and vivid is the ability word that cares about it. A vivid ability counts the number of colours among permanents you control, from zero to five, and uses that number somehow. Lands are colourless, so they do not count unless something gives them a colour. A cycle of uncommon elementals all carry vivid, {Shinestriker} among them, and {Aurora Awakener} is a seven-mana giant that, as it enters, reveals permanents from your library until it finds one for each of your colours and puts any of them onto the battlefield.",
                        "Shadowmoor's half is hybrid mana, as in the old Shadowmoor block. A hybrid symbol can be paid with either colour, but the card is always every colour in its cost, so {Abigale, Eloquent First-Year} is white and black even if you cast her with only black mana. Hybrid runs all through the set, from a common creature for each family, like {Wary Farmer} for the kithkin, to the five Strixhaven students, a cycle of rare hybrid legends in the enemy colour pairs.",
                        "The set's most clever hybrid cards are the five mythic Elemental Incarnations. Each has a hybrid cost and a hybrid evoke cost, and two abilities on entering, each of which only happens if two mana of one particular colour was spent. Evoke one for one colour and you get a cheap, single effect. Pay the full price with both colours and you get both. They are a callback to the evoke Incarnations of the original Lorwyn.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-kindred", "Kindred and Changeling",
                    listOf(
                        "The original Lorwyn was famous for its creature-type families, and about half this set leans on them. Kithkin are green and white, merfolk white and blue, goblins black and red, elementals blue and red, and elves black and green. Giants and treefolk have smaller themes of their own, and faeries a minor one in the blue and black flash deck. Behold returns from Tarkir Dragonstorm, letting a card check for a creature type by pointing at a permanent you control or simply revealing a card from your hand. The set also restores the creature type Sorcerer as red's spellcaster, starting with Ashling, and handed it to about two dozen older creatures named as sorcerers.",
                        "Changeling returns as the glue. A card with changeling is every creature type everywhere, in hand, library or graveyard, so it is a Merfolk for your merfolk and a Goblin for your goblins. Two old changeling instants are reprinted, {Crib Swap} and {Nameless Inversion}, and there is a cycle of uncommon changelings and a cycle of common hybrid ones.",
                        "Kindred, the card type Lorwyn first introduced as Tribal, was retired years ago, but designers felt the plane where it began was the right place to use it once more. A kindred card is a noncreature card with creature types, so {Boggart Mischief} is a Goblin card in your library and graveyard, though it is not a creature on the battlefield. The five rare Commands, one per family, are all kindred, and the first mode of each copies a permanent of its type. The uncommon cycle of kindred spells includes {Wanderwine Farewell}, which only makes its merfolk tokens if you control a Merfolk other than itself.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-transforming", "Shifting Legends",
                    listOf(
                        "Nothing captures Lorwyn-Shadowmoor like a card with two faces. A few key characters are double-faced cards with a transform ability that triggers at the beginning of your first main phase. If you pay a single mana of the other colour, the card turns over to its other aspect, and next turn you can pay the first colour to turn it back. This gives you more control over transforming than most sets allow, and the designers aimed for each face to be worth about the same.",
                        "The five family leaders form the rare cycle. Brigid of the kithkin turns from white to green, Sygg of the merrow from blue to white, Grub of the boggarts from black to red, Ashling of the flamekin from red to blue, and Trystan of the elves from green to black. Each face is a different version of the character, so {Grub, Storied Matriarch} brings goblins back from your graveyard by day, and as Grub, Notorious Auntie she blights one of your creatures whenever she attacks to send a temporary copy of it into the fight.",
                        "Two mythic pairs use the same frame. {Eirdu, Carrier of Dawn} is a white flier with lifelink that gives your creature spells convoke, and flips for black into Isilu, Carrier of Twilight, which gives your other creatures persist. {Oko, Lorwyn Liege} is a blue planeswalker that turns green as Oko, Shadowmoor Scion. A back face has no mana cost of its own, so its mana value is that of its front.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-old-keywords", "Old Keywords",
                    listOf(
                        "Several old mechanics come back. Convoke appears in a big way, letting you tap creatures to help pay for a spell, each creature paying for one generic mana or one mana of its colour. Each of the five rare typal-support spells has convoke and rewards a chosen creature type, and Eirdu hands convoke to all your creature spells. Persist, wither and proliferate, all Shadowmoor favourites, appear only on a handful of cards, Isilu and Rhys for persist, {Spinerock Tyrant} for wither and Morcant for proliferate, but each sits right on top of the -1/-1 counter theme.",
                        "Evoke is on the Incarnations. Champion, the old Lorwyn ability that exiled one of your creatures until the champion left, is not keyworded, but a rare cycle recalls it, each card requiring you to behold a creature of the right family and exile it, then giving it back when the champion leaves. {Champions of the Perfect} is the elf one, and draws a card whenever you cast a creature spell, while {Champions of the Shoal} leads the merfolk.",
                        "One strange little first sits among the giants. {Goliath Daydreamer} exiles your instants and sorceries with dream counters on them as they resolve, and whenever it attacks you may cast one of those dreams again for free. It is the first card to use dream counters that has nothing to do with Rasputin Dreamweaver, the old Legends card that introduced them. Over in the Commander decks, encore returns on a cycle of elementals, letting each come back from the graveyard as a hasty copy attacking every opponent at once.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-archetypes", "Draft Archetypes",
                    listOf(
                        "The set's ten two-colour draft pairs split neatly. Five follow the creature families, each with its own mechanical twist, as Bloomburrow did before. White and blue merfolk care about tapping your creatures. Black and red goblins use -1/-1 counters and sacrifice. Green and white kithkin go wide and reward playing kithkin. Blue and red elementals care about spells of mana value four or more. Black and green elves care about elves in your graveyard.",
                        "The other five are mechanical. Blue and black is flash control with a minor faerie theme. Red and green is vivid midrange, and green and blue is vivid ramp. White and black is blight control, and red and white is blight aggro. Every one of the ten has a hybrid signpost, and the family archetypes have a gold signpost too. The uncommon hybrid signposts for the five mechanical pairs each show one of the plane's rarer peoples, a treefolk, an ouphe, a faerie, a giant and a noggle.",
                        "The set came with a new product, Draft Night, a box with twelve Play Boosters, a Collector Booster, ninety basic lands and ten double-sided tokens, enough for a group to run a Pick-Two Draft straight out of the box. Every card in a Play Booster, Special Guests included, is legal in draft and sealed.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Lorwyn Eclipsed"),
                StorySource("Story", "Lorwyn Eclipsed Mechanics, by Matt Tabak, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Lorwyn Eclipsed"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "ecl-cards", "Cards Worth Knowing",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-incarnations", "The Incarnations",
                    listOf(
                        "The five mythic hybrid Incarnations are the set's headline cycle. {Emptiness} in white and black returns a cheap creature from your graveyard if you paid two white, and puts three -1/-1 counters on a creature if you paid two black. {Wistfulness} in green and blue exiles an opponent's artifact or enchantment for green and draws two then discards one for blue. {Deceit} in blue and black bounces a nonland permanent for blue and strips a card from an opponent's hand for black.",
                        "{Catharsis} in red and white makes two kithkin tokens for white and pumps your team with haste for red. {Vibrance} in red and green deals three damage for red and fetches a land and gains life for green. Each can be evoked for two hybrid mana to grab one half cheaply, and because evoke sacrifices the creature, they fit any deck that likes creatures entering and leaving.",
                        "The Commander decks added a second cycle of monocoloured Incarnations with encore, which gets its own section in the Commander tab. Both cycles look back to the original Lorwyn, where a cycle of elemental Incarnations and a run of evoke elementals first appeared, and two of those old evoke elementals, Mulldrifter and Shriekmaw, are reprinted in the Dance of the Elements deck. In the story's world the greater elementals are living ideas, and these cards are exactly that, Emptiness and Wistfulness and Deceit given bodies and set loose.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-mythics", "Mythics and Bombs",
                    listOf(
                        "{Bitterbloom Bearer} is the set's headliner, a two-mana black faerie flier that costs you a life every upkeep and makes a 1/1 blue and black faerie with flying. Its serialized version, numbered to five hundred, has art by Rebecca Guay, who painted the original Lorwyn faeries, in the fable frame, with double rainbow foil. {Ajani, Outland Chaperone} is a three-mana white planeswalker who makes kithkin tokens and deals four damage to a tapped creature.",
                        "{Glen Elendra's Answer} is a four-mana instant that cannot be countered and counters every spell and ability your opponents control, making a faerie for each. {Mornsong Aria} stops all players drawing cards or gaining life, and instead each player pays three life every draw step to tutor any card. {Morningtide's Light} exiles any number of creatures and returns them at the next end step, while preventing all damage to you until your next turn. {Mirrorform} turns every nonland permanent you control into a copy of one target permanent. {Meek Attack} lets you drop small creatures from your hand with haste for a turn.",
                        "Among the rares, {Chronicle of Victory} is a six-mana legendary artifact that gives a chosen creature type +2/+2, first strike and trample and draws you a card whenever you cast a spell of that type. {Celestial Reunion} tutors a creature, and puts it straight onto the battlefield if you beheld two creatures of its type. {Loch Mare} draws cards and stuns creatures by removing its own -1/-1 counters, and {Doran, Besieged by Time} reprises the old treefolk's toughness theme in three colours.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Petals burst in evening hours with flitting things so fickle and sour.",
                        "Bitterbloom Bearer",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-cycles", "Cycles",
                    listOf(
                        "Each of the five families gets a cycle at almost every rarity. The rare Commands, {Brigid's Command}, {Sygg's Command}, {Grub's Command}, {Ashling's Command} and {Trystan's Command}, are named for the transforming legends. The rare Champions, like {Champion of the Clachan}, are cheap for their stats but demand you behold and exile a creature of their family. The Eclipsed creatures, like {Eclipsed Kithkin}, are hybrid scouts that dig the top four cards for a creature of their type or a basic land.",
                        "There are Beholders, undercosted uncommons that need either two extra mana or a beheld card, gold signposts, kindred uncommons and hybrid commons for each family. Outside the families there are rare typal convoke spells, five allied-colour rares, two common elementals with basic landcycling, two giants that care about giants, and two colourless uncommons, {Eclipsed Realms} and {Dawn-Blessed Pennant}, that each name one of eight creature types as they enter.",
                        "Mirrored pairs run through the set too. {Crib Swap} and {Nameless Inversion} are the two reprinted changeling instants, one white and one black. {Reluctant Dounguard} and {Heirloom Auntie} are both 4/4s for three that enter with two -1/-1 counters, which the kithkin sheds as your other creatures enter and the boggart sheds as your other creatures die.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-shock-lands", "The Shock Lands",
                    listOf(
                        "The set reprints the five allied shock lands, completing for Standard the cycle that Edge of Eternities began. Each one's art shows the home of one of the set's families on the border between bright Lorwyn on the left and dark Shadowmoor on the right, and their flavour text is a matched set. Each describes how its people behave in the light, then the words when the skies darkened, then what they do in the dark.",
                        "{Temple Garden} belongs to the kithkin, admiring the flowers until the skies darken and then ripping up the roots for next season. {Hallowed Fountain} is the merrow, vending her wares and then raising her prices. {Blood Crypt} is a boggart prodding a critter, then gobbling it down with glee. {Steam Vents} is a flamekin drawn toward warm laughter who turns back to her lonely peak. {Overgrown Tomb} is an elf disgusted by a bramble who steps closer to see its wild beauty.",
                        "In Booster Fun the shock lands are borderless and reversible, with the Lorwyn side of the home on one face and the Shadowmoor side on the other.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "The elf was disgusted by the bramble before him when the skies darkened, then he stepped closer to observe the wild beauty.",
                        "Overgrown Tomb",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-booster-fun", "Booster Fun",
                    listOf(
                        "The main set has 283 cards, 273 regular cards plus two sets of full-art basic lands, one Lorwyn and one Shadowmoor. The showcase treatment is the fable frame, which turns up on fifty cards, and there are borderless cards, extended-art cards, the reversible shock lands and the serialized headliner. Collector Boosters also carry Japan Showcase cards, some in fracture foil. Kinbinding was the alternate-art bundle promo and Harmonized Crescendo the Buy-a-Box promo.",
                        "The Special Guests for the set were twenty reprints in a fairy-tale papercraft style, ten Lorwyn-themed and ten Shadowmoor-themed, including {Mistbind Clique}, {Door of Destinies} and {Devoted Druid}. They can turn up in Play Boosters in place of a common and are legal in draft. Prerelease promos dropped the date stamp, ending a tradition twenty-eight years old.",
                        "Organised play brought more. Standard Showdown gave a retro-frame Nowhere to Run, Commander events a retro-frame Farhaven Elf, and Store Championships a full-art {Goblin Bombardment} for playing. The Magic Spotlight event for the set was called Eclipsed and gave a textless {Vendilion Clique}. Two sixty-card Theme Decks, one of pirates and one of angels, came out alongside the set too, though they were not themed to the plane.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Lorwyn Eclipsed"),
                StorySource("Story", "Collecting Lorwyn Eclipsed, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Lorwyn Eclipsed"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "ecl-commander", "Commander Decks",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-dance-of-elements", "Dance of the Elements",
                    listOf(
                        "Two Commander decks came out with the set under the code ECC, each with twelve new cards, a foil borderless commander and a second foil borderless legend. Dance of the Elements is the five-colour deck and stands for the light of Lorwyn. Its commander is {Ashling, the Limitless}, a three-mana red flamekin who gives every elemental permanent spell you cast from your hand evoke for four, and whenever you sacrifice a nontoken elemental makes a hasty token copy of it that you keep only if you pay one mana of every colour at your next end step. She was given an errata on release day so that only elemental permanent spells gain evoke, and so that removing her mid-cast no longer stops the sacrifice.",
                        "So the deck evokes huge elementals for four, gets their enter abilities, sacrifices them, and gets them back as tokens. Its second legend is {Mass of Mysteries}, a five-colour first strike, vigilance and trample elemental that gives another elemental myriad each combat. The rest of the deck is a who's who of elementals and Lorwyn nostalgia, Mulldrifter, Shriekmaw, Horde of Notions, Omnath, Locus of Rage, Maelstrom Wanderer and Risen Reef among them.",
                        "Its other new cards include {Elemental Spectacle}, which makes a 5/5 elemental for each of your colours, {Springleaf Parade}, which makes changelings and turns every creature token into a mana source, and the land {Abundant Countryside}. It also prints, for the first time, the green and blue members of two old land cycles that had only ever existed in allied pairs, the bicycle land {Rain-Slicked Copse} and the battle land {Sodden Verdure}.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-blight-curse", "Blight Curse",
                    listOf(
                        "Blight Curse is black, red and green and stands for the darkness of Shadowmoor. It is built around -1/-1 counters, draining and creature combos. Its commander is {Auntie Ool, Cursewretch}, whose ward costs an opponent a blight of two, and who draws you a card whenever -1/-1 counters land on your own creature and drains a life from the controller whenever they land on someone else's. Its second legend is {The Reaper, King No More}, a scarecrow artifact creature whose cost can be paid in its colours or with two generic mana per symbol, and that spreads counters and steals opposing creatures that die while wearing them.",
                        "The deck's new cards lean into counters from every angle. {Ferrafor, Young Yew} makes a saproling for every counter on a player's creatures and can double every counter on a creature. {Oft-Nabbed Goat} lets opponents pay to draw a card and steal it, picking up a -1/-1 counter each time, and when it dies its owner draws that many cards. {Village Pillagers} has wither and makes Treasure whenever an opposing creature with a counter on it dies, and {Wickersmith's Tools} turns all those counters into scarecrows.",
                        "The rest of the new cards play the same game. {Dread Tiller} drops lands when creatures wearing -1/-1 counters die, {Puca's Covenant} returns permanents from your graveyard, {Eventide's Shadow} removes any number of counters to draw that many cards, {Sinister Gnarlbark} draws and blights every turn, {Aberrant Return} reanimates up to three creatures with an extra counter each, and {Grave Venerations} makes you the monarch. The deck shipped with a punch card for tracking -1/-1 counters and poison.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Ajani's heart went out to those changed by the aurora's tides. Having selfhood ripped away was still a painfully familiar feeling.",
                        "Eventide's Shadow",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "ecl-encore-cycle", "Encore Incarnations",
                    listOf(
                        "Dance of the Elements holds the Commander product's one new cycle, five monocoloured Elemental Incarnations with an enter ability and encore. Encore lets you exile the card from your graveyard to make a hasty token copy attacking each opponent, which is sacrificed at end of turn, so in multiplayer every enter ability fires once per opponent.",
                        "{Belonging} in white makes three changeling tokens. {Subterfuge} in blue gives a creature flying and makes it draw a card for every point of combat damage it deals to a player. {Lamentation} in black destroys an opposing creature and gains three life. {Impulsivity} in red casts an instant or sorcery from any graveyard for free. {Jubilation} in green gives your creatures +2/+2 and trample.",
                        "With Ashling at the helm the cycle doubles up. Evoke any of them for four, get the effect, sacrifice it and get a hasty copy, then later encore it from the graveyard for another round against every opponent. Like the set's own Incarnations, they are named for feelings and ideas, Belonging, Subterfuge, Lamentation, Impulsivity and Jubilation, the plane's greater elementals given card form.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Lorwyn Eclipsed Commander"),
                StorySource("Story", "Lorwyn Eclipsed Commander Decklists, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Lorwyn Eclipsed Commander"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
    ),
)
